Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Steps 1-2: SINR link model and emergent reachability with
 real routing

Step 1 - physics, not fudge:
- rf_link(): log-distance path loss + Friis reference, SINR with an
  interference term, logistic PDR. One currency for range, walls and (next)
  jamming; a jammer becomes another term in the denominator rather than a
  special case. Sanity: -20 dBm at 1 m, sensitivity ~200 m, dead by 500 m.
- link_state() now delegates to it, so every caller gets real physics.
- links are CANDIDATES derived from network membership only. The old code
  built them from whether a coordinator existed, so routing was derived from
  the declaration and could never disagree with it - topology was unmeasurable.

Step 2 - routing, and measuring whether it is real:
- apply_routing(): star / mesh / tiered decide which reachable candidates are
  ACTIVE. Links now carry active (used) and usable (physically up), so spare
  capacity is visible - the thing a mesh has and a star does not.
- observed_topology(): betweenness over pairs that have an intermediate,
  reported next to the declared routing so the tool can say 'you declared
  mesh, the graph says star'.
- verified: star->star (1.00, hub gcs), mesh->mesh (0.00), tiered with one
  squad -> star on the leader (correct: one squad IS a star), tiered with two
  squads -> mixed (0.667, gcs on top, leaders relaying). Cascading comms
  lines, computed rather than declared.
- fixed betweenness normalisation: counting adjacent pairs capped a 4-node
  star at 0.5 and made it indistinguishable from a partial mesh.
